<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Ostorius Scapula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 52</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Ostorius Scapula was Roman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 47 to 52. He tried to disarm the tribes behind the frontier, provoking a revolt of the Iceni in 47, campaigned against the Deceangli and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> in 51. Worn out by the resistance of the Silures, he died in office in 52.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula.jpg" alt="Publius Ostorius Scapula" width="320" height="739" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ad Meskens · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_baths_ostorius_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ostorius was the son of a Publius and probably the nephew of Quintus Ostorius Scapula, one of the first two commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> under Augustus and later prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; the Quintus Ostorius Scapula who was consul in 41 was probably his brother or cousin. Nothing is known of his early career. He was suffect consul, probably in 46, and in the winter of 47 the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> made him the second governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, in succession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tribes outside Roman control attacked, expecting a new governor to avoid a late campaign, but Ostorius struck back at once. He apparently announced that he would disarm all the Britons south and east of the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> and Severn, which has fed debate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fosse-way">Fosse Way</a> as a frontier. The Iceni, allies rather than subjects, led a rising that he crushed by storming a hill fort, possibly Stonea Camp in the Fens; his son Marcus won the corona civica in the fighting. In 48 he campaigned against the Deceangli of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, separating the northern tribes from those of Wales, before a revolt among the Brigantes called him back.</p><p>In the safer south he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first colony of veterans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>, in 49 and probably a municipium at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>. Caratacus, now leading the Silures and then the Ordovices, was forced after years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war into battle, probably near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a>. Tacitus records that the eagerness of the troops persuaded Ostorius to attack a fortified ridge, and the Britons were easily beaten. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> of the Brigantes later handed the fugitive Caratacus over in chains, and Ostorius received the triumphal insignia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Silures fought on, reportedly after Ostorius said publicly that they should be exterminated or transplanted; they besieged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> force building forts and bound neighbouring tribes to them by sharing Roman prisoners as hostages. The historian Peter Salway attributes this to the governor's lack of political judgement. Ostorius died suddenly in 52, worn out with care in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>'s words. The raids continued, defeating a legion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manlius-valens">Gaius Manlius Valens</a>, and the area was pacified only 25 years later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-julius-frontinus">Sextus Julius Frontinus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Publius Ostorius Scapula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 48 the governor <b>Ostorius Scapula</b> marched against the Deceangli, ravaging their land almost to the Irish Sea before trouble among the Brigantes recalled him.</blockquote><a href="/state/deceangli">Deceangli</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Roman conquest of 43 the Iceni became a client kingdom, but they rebelled in 47 when the governor <b>Ostorius Scapula</b> tried to disarm them.</blockquote><a href="/state/iceni">Iceni</a></li><li><blockquote>Caratacus fought alongside them after 47, and in 52 they defeated a legion under Manlius Valens; the governor <b>Ostorius Scapula</b> died worn out by the campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/silures">Silures</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Britannia, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.33,-0.59&amp;year=42" aria-label="Britannia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Publius Ostorius Scapula"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4969 5654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4969 5654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.33,-0.59&amp;year=42">What was here in 42 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
